  data:  # 48f2
    kind: invariant  # 48f2
    title: "Source-text string captured as identifier value in one language's analyzer"  # 48f2
    status: violated  # 48f2
    priority: 2  # 48f2
    parent:  # 48f2
    tags:  # 48f2
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # 48f2
    - dogfood_integrated  # 48f2
    - dogfood_severity_moderate  # 48f2
    isbefore: []  # 48f2
    duplicate_of: []  # 48f2
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 48f2
    pr_ref:  # 48f2
    description: "The Solidity analyzer treats arbitrary string literals — specifically `require()` error messages — as import targets. When the analyzer encounters a runtime-check function whose argument is a string literal, it captures the message string as if it were an import path. The synthesized record carries the message string in fields meant to hold language and identifier values; downstream linkers propagate the string into multiple derived fields and one cross-cutting output view. A single ingestion-point bug contaminates the language axis, the canonical-name axis, and the unsupported-languages list of `io-boundaries`.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) Solidity analyzer emits `imports` edges and `external_symbol` nodes for `require()` revert-message string literals.** On hypergumbo self-analysis (branch `dev @ 77a69627b8`), the literal `\"Not owner\"` from `tests/fixtures/schema-coverage-corpus/solidity/Contract.sol:18` produces a bogus `external_symbol` node (`{id: 'Not owner:<unknown>:0-0:Not owner:Not owner', kind: external_symbol, language: 'Not owner', name: 'Not owner', path: '<external>'}`) plus a bogus `imports` edge (`solidity:.../Contract.sol --imports--> 'Not owner:...'`). Root cause: `packages/hypergumbo-lang-extended1/src/hypergumbo_lang_extended1/solidity.py:355-369` iterates every node in the tree-sitter parse tree and unconditionally calls `_extract_import_aliases(node, source)`. That helper looks for any direct `\"string\"` child via `find_child_by_type(node, \"string\")`; if found, the string contents become the import path. Tree nodes like `call_argument` (which wraps `require`'s arguments) have a direct `\"string\"` child, so the function treats them as imports. The indentation on lines 357-369 is 12 spaces deep inside an 8-space-block `for`, suggesting an earlier `if node.type == \"import_directive\":` guard was removed without re-indenting the body.\n\n**(2) The string contaminates `node.language` and propagates to `io-boundaries --json` and `verify-claims` diagnostics.** The synthesized `external_symbol` for `\"Not owner\"` carries `language='Not owner'` as the literal value (using the source string as the language detection result). That value propagates to `io-boundaries.unsupported_languages = ['Not owner', 'ansible', 'bash', 'csharp', ...]` — a multi-word natural-language string appears in the unsupported-languages list. It also leaks into the `verify-claims` `INV-javam` diagnostic: `Note: no taint-flow catalog for language(s): Not owner, ansible, ...`. The `id` format `<lang>:<path>:<span>:<name>:<kind>` with `lang=path=name='Not owner'` shows the analyzer had no language-detection result and defaulted to the source-of-the-string value across the whole identity record.\n\n**(3) The same string also leaks into `node.canonical_name` as a `:Not owner` suffix — contamination crosses to a second axis.** A scan of distinct `canonical_name` \"state suffix\" (last colon segment) values returns: `{'unresolved': 1295, 'attribute': 213, 'doc_link': 102, 'module': 11, 'script': 1, 'Not owner': 1, 'package': 1}`. `Not owner` appears as a canonical_name state suffix — the Solidity require-msg leaks into the canonical-name axis too, not just `node.language`. The single affected node carries `language='Not owner'` AND `canonical_name endswith ':Not owner'`. The unresolved-symbol synthesis path stores the source string as both `language` and the canonical_name suffix; the same garbage propagates across both axes from one ingestion point.\n\n### Invariant\n\nAnalyzer-emitted `language` and identifier values should be drawn from the known-language catalog and the canonical identifier vocabulary. When an analyzer cannot resolve an identifier, it should route through a documented \"unknown\" sentinel or omit the record rather than synthesizing it from arbitrary source text. The `imports` edge emitter in the Solidity analyzer should only fire on `import_directive` nodes, not on every tree node with a string child. Source-text strings extracted from one syntactic context must not propagate into more than one node-identity axis.\n\n### Scope\n\nSingle-instance in the observed dataset (one node), but the synthesizing code path applies to every source occurrence of the affected pattern in Solidity (i.e., every `require()`, `assert()`, `revert()` with a string-literal argument). The single-node footprint propagates through `node.language`, `node.canonical_name` suffix, `io-boundaries.unsupported_languages`, and `verify-claims` diagnostics — four affected surfaces from one ingestion.\n\n### Exposure\n\nBehavior-map `node.language` field, `node.canonical_name` field, `io-boundaries.unsupported_languages` output view, `verify-claims` diagnostic strings.\n\n### Consequence\n\nType-safety on the `language` field is violated — consumers expecting an enumerated language value receive arbitrary source text. The contamination crosses field boundaries: a single analyzer bug propagates into multiple seemingly-unrelated fields and views. `io-boundaries` reports a multi-word natural-language string as an \"unsupported language\" alongside real language names; downstream tooling parsing the unsupported-languages list gets corrupted data.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Restore the `if node.type == \"import_directive\": continue/else` guard at `solidity.py:355-369` so `_extract_import_aliases` runs only on actual import directives, not on every tree node with a string child.\n- For unresolved-symbol synthesis, route through an `unknown` sentinel for `language` rather than defaulting to the source string. Apply the same to the `canonical_name` suffix.\n- Add a validator at the analyzer-emission boundary that rejects `language` values not in the known-language catalog (or routes them to `unknown` with a warning).\n- Add a property test that asserts no `external_symbol` node's `language` value contains whitespace (a multi-word language value is structurally impossible).\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-hojak, INV-kalaf, INV-domig — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 48f2
    fields:  # 48f2
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-2ba53e"  # 48f2
      statement: "Analyzer-emitted language and identifier values should be drawn from the known-language catalog and canonical identifier vocabulary. When an analyzer cannot resolve an identifier, it should route through a documented \"unknown\" sentinel or omit the record rather than synthesizing it from arbitrary source text."  # 48f2
      root_cause: "One language's analyzer treats certain source-text strings as identifier targets. Specifically, when the analyzer encounters a runtime-check function whose argument is a string literal intended as an error message, it captures the message string as if it were an import or symbol identifier. The synthesized record then carries the message string in fields that should hold language and identifier values. Downstream linkers consuming the language field propagate the string into derived fields including a canonical-name suffix and the unsupported-languages list of an output view. One node ends up carrying a multi-word natural-language string as its language value."  # 48f2
